Part C: Viva questions and answers
Practical 1
Q: What does a correlation coefficient measure?
A: The strength and direction of the linear relationship between two numeric variables, from -1 to +1. Zero means no linear relationship.
Q: Why select only numeric columns?
A: corr() needs numbers. Text columns such as IDs or names cannot be correlated.
Q: What does a histogram show?
A: The distribution of one variable: its shape, spread, skew and peaks.
Q: Does high correlation mean causation?
A: No. Two variables can move together without one causing the other.
Q: What do the heatmap colours mean?
A: With coolwarm, red is a positive correlation and blue is negative. annot=True prints the value in each cell.
Practical 2
Q: Why drop duplicate rows?
A: Duplicates count the same customer twice and bias averages and counts.
Q: What does errors="coerce" do?
A: Values that cannot be converted, like "XXXX", become NaN instead of raising an error.
Q: Why fill with the median instead of the mean?
A: The median is not pulled by outliers. Use mode for categorical columns.
Q: Label encoding or one-hot encoding?
A: Label encoding gives integer codes, fine for binary or ordered categories. One-hot suits unordered categories with many values, because integers would imply a false order.
Q: How does a box plot show outliers?
A: Points beyond Q1 - 1.5 x IQR or Q3 + 1.5 x IQR are drawn as individual dots.
Q: Why parse dates?
A: Text dates cannot be sorted or subtracted. datetime lets you extract year and month and compute durations.
Practical 3
Q: Simple versus stratified sampling?
A: In simple random sampling every row has an equal chance. In stratified sampling you split the data into groups (strata) and sample from each group, usually in proportion to its size.
Q: Why stratify penguins by species?
A: Body mass differs a lot between species, so stratifying guarantees every species is represented and usually gives a more stable estimate.
Q: What is random_state?
A: A seed that makes the random sample repeatable.
Q: Why could the stratified sample have 101 rows?
A: Each species count is rounded separately, and the rounded numbers can add up to slightly more or less than 100.
Q: Is stratified always more accurate?
A: Usually closer to the population mean, but one random draw can still be off, so say 'usually'.
Practical 4
Q: State the Central Limit Theorem.
A: For a large enough sample size, the distribution of sample means is approximately normal with mean mu and standard deviation sigma / sqrt(n), whatever the shape of the population (finite variance).
Q: Why use an exponential distribution?
A: It is clearly right-skewed, so the move to a bell shape shows the theorem working.
Q: What are the expected values here?
A: Mean about 2 and standard deviation of sample means about 2 / sqrt(30) = 0.365.
Q: What happens if the sample size grows?
A: The distribution of means becomes narrower and closer to normal.
Q: Why n = 30?
A: A common rule of thumb for 'large enough'. Very skewed data may need more.
Practical 5
Q: What is bootstrap resampling?
A: Repeatedly resampling the observed data with replacement to estimate the sampling distribution of a statistic.
Q: Why replace=True?
A: Without replacement every resample would be the original data in a different order. Replacement lets some values repeat and others drop out.
Q: Why is each resample as large as the data?
A: So the variability mimics real sampling from a dataset of that size.
Q: How is the 95% CI found?
A: Take the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles of the 5000 bootstrap means.
Q: Main advantage?
A: No assumption about the population distribution, and it works for statistics with no simple formula.
Practical 6
Q: What is maximum likelihood estimation?
A: Choosing the parameter values that make the observed data most probable.
Q: Give the normal MLE formulas.
A: mu-hat = (1/n) sum x. sigma-squared-hat = (1/n) sum (x - mu-hat)^2.
Q: Why divide by n and not n - 1?
A: The MLE variance is slightly biased. n - 1 gives the unbiased sample variance. The gap is tiny for large n.
Q: Why use log-likelihood?
A: It turns a product of probabilities into a sum, which is easier to differentiate and avoids numerical underflow.
Q: What assumption did you make?
A: That petal length follows a normal distribution.
Practical 7
Q: What is K-Means?
A: An unsupervised algorithm. It assigns each point to the nearest centroid, recomputes the centroids, and repeats until stable, minimising within-cluster distance.
Q: Why scale the features?
A: Salary is in tens of thousands and age in tens. Without scaling, distance depends almost only on salary.
Q: How do you choose k?
A: Elbow method or silhouette score. k = 3 here was chosen, not proven optimal.
Q: What do n_init and random_state do?
A: n_init runs K-Means from several random starts and keeps the best. random_state makes it repeatable.
Q: Is Purchased used for clustering?
A: No. Clustering is unsupervised. Purchased is only used afterwards to describe each cluster.
